revision.text:
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷreh₂-}}
The {{glossary|adjective}} is {{etydate|1471|nocap=1}} in {{w|Middle English}}, the {{glossary|verb}} in 1530; from {{lbor|en|la|aggravātus|notext=1}}, {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|aggravō||to add to the weight of, make worse, oppress, annoy}} (see {{af|en|-ate|id1=verb|pos1=verb-forming suffix}} and {{af|en|-ate|id1=adjective|pos1=adjective-forming suffix}}), from {{m|la|ad-||to}} + {{m|la|gravō||to make heavy}}, from {{m|la|gravis||heavy}} + {{m|la|-ō|id=denominative|pos=verb-forming suffix}}. See {{m|en|grave}} and compare {{m|en|aggrieve}} and {{m|en|aggrege}}. Participial usage of the adjective up until {{w|Early Modern English}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæɡ.ɹə.veɪ̯t/<q:verb>}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Mélange a trois-aggravate.wav|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæɡɹəvət/<q:adjective>}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To make (an offence) worse or more severe; to increase in offensiveness or heinousness. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|add insult to injury|twist the knife|Thesaurus:make matters worse}}
#: {{ant|en|pour oil on troubled waters|alleviate}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|I|i|23|2|Once more, the more to '''aggrauate''' the note,<br>With a foule Traitors name ſtuffe I thy throte,<br>And wiſh (ſo pleaſe my Soueraigne) ere I moue,<br>What my tong ſpeaks, my right drawn ſword may proue}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1709|author=w:Joseph Addison|title=The Tatler
|passage=The defense made by the prisoner's counsel did rather '''aggravate''' than extenuate his crime.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} To make (any bad thing) worse. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|exacerbate|Thesaurus:aggravate}}
#: {{ant|en|alleviate|mitigate}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|volume=IV|book=XVI|passage=to '''aggravate''' my woes.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1837|author=w:William H. Prescott|title=History of the Reign of Ferdinand and Isabella the Catholic
|passage={{...}} to '''aggravate''' the horrors of the scene}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} To give extra [[weight]] or [[intensity]] to; to exaggerate, to [[magnify]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|heighten|intensify|increase|magnify|Thesaurus:increase}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''aggravated''' the story.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[pile]] or [[heap]] (something heavy or onerous) {{m|en|on}} or {{m|en|upon}} someone. {{defdate|16th–18th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|dogpile|heap on|pile on}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1790|author=w:Edmund Burke|title=Reflections on the Revolution in France|page=28|publisher=Oxford|year_published=2009
|passage=In order to lighten the crown still further, they '''aggravated''' responsibility on ministers of state.}}
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|colloquial|;|often|proscribed|;|see usage notes}} To [[exasperate]]; to [[provoke]] or [[irritate]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|provoke|irritate|exasperate|Thesaurus:annoy}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|passage=If both were to '''aggravate''' her parents, as my brother and sister do mine.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977|author=w:Alistair Horne|title=A Savage War of Peace|location=New York|publisher=Review Books|year_published=2006|page=85
|passage=Ben Bella was '''aggravated''' by having to express himself in French because the Egyptians were unable to understand his Arabic.}}

====Usage notes====
* Although the meaning "to exasperate, to annoy" has been in continuous usage since the 16th century, a large number of usage [[maven]]s have contested it since the 1870s. Opinions have swayed from this proscription since 1965, but it still garners disapproval in ''Garner's Modern American Usage'' (2009), at least for formal writing.

====Derived terms====
{{col2|en|reaggravate|aggravatable|aggravative|aggravator|aggro|overaggravate}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|aggravation}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|To make worse, or more severe}}
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|влошавам}}, {{t+|bg|утежнявам}}
* Catalan: {{t+|ca|agreujar}}
* Czech: {{t+|cs|zhoršit|pf}}, {{t|cs|ztížit|pf}}, {{t|cs|zostřit|pf}}
* Danish: {{t+|da|forværre}}, {{t+|da|f}}
* Dutch: {{t+|nl|verergeren}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|pahentaa}}
* French: {{t+|fr|aggraver}}
* German: {{t+|de|verschlimmern}}, {{t+|de|erschweren}}
* Hungarian: {{t+|hu|súlyosbít}}
* Latin: {{t|la|aggravō}}
* Malayalam: {{t|ml|വഷളാക്കുക}}
* Norwegian:
*: Bokmål: {{t|nb|forverre}}
*: Nynorsk: {{t|nn|forverre}}
* Occitan: {{t|oc|agravar}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|agravar}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|обостря́ть|impf}}, {{t+|ru|обостри́ть|pf}}, {{t+|ru|усугубля́ть|impf}}, {{t+|ru|усугуби́ть|pf}}, {{t+|ru|отягощать|impf}}, {{t+|ru|ухудшать|impf}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|agravar}}, {{t+|es|empeorar}}
* Ukrainian: {{t|uk|погіршувати}}, {{t|uk|загострювати}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|To give coloring to in description; to exaggerate; as, to aggravate circumstances}}
* Czech: {{t|cs|přibarvit|pf}}
* Malayalam: {{t|ml|ഊതിപ്പെരുപ്പിക്കുക}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|усложня́ть|impf}}
* Ukrainian: {{t|uk|ускладнювати}}, {{t+|uk|обтяжувати}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|To exasperate; to provoke; to irritate}}
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|ядосвам}}
* Catalan: {{t+|ca|irritar}}
* Czech: {{t|cs|rozčílit|pf}}, {{t+|cs|rozčilovat|impf}}, {{t+|cs|štvát|impf}}
* Danish: {{t|da|fremprovokere}}
* Esperanto: {{t+|eo|agaci}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|ärsyttää}}, {{t+|fi|suututtaa}}
* German: {{t+|de|ärgern}}, {{t+|de|verbittern}}
* Malayalam: {{t|ml|അലോസരപ്പെടുത്തുക}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|abusar}}, {{t+|pt|provocar}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|раздража́ть|impf}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|irritar}}, {{t+|es|exasperar}}, {{t+|es|sublevar}}
* Ukrainian: {{t|uk|дратувати}}
{{trans-bottom}}

